# Copy to `.env` for local development only.
# Expo loads `.env` automatically via `expo start`. Variables prefixed with
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_` are inlined into the JS bundle at build time — same pattern
# as the web app's `VITE_` vars (see architecture.md §2.1).
#
# These are host URLs, not secrets — safe to ship in the client bundle.
# Do NOT put backend credentials, Supabase keys, or SMTP tokens here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development (default)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend API host with NO path suffix. The client appends `/api/v1/...`
# itself (api-design.md §6 — the `/api` prefix and `/v1` mount are fixed).
#
# Point at a locally running backend (see ../backend/README.md):
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
#
# Physical device on the same LAN (replace with your machine's IP):
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.42:3000
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Staging / preview builds (EAS `preview` profile)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do NOT commit staging or production URLs in this file.
# Set the value in EAS instead — it is scoped to the `preview` environment:
#
#   eas env:create --name EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL \
#     --value "https://td-it-solution-insurance.onrender.com" \
#     --environment preview --visibility plaintext

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production store builds (EAS `production` profile)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set in EAS, scoped to the `production` environment:
#
#   eas env:create --name EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL \
#     --value "https://td-it-solution-insurance.onrender.com" \
#     --environment production --visibility plaintext
#
# Before the first production build, confirm:
#   - The Render backend is deployed and `/api/health/ready` returns 200.
#   - Render's `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` includes your app origins (mobile apps
#     typically do not send an Origin header on native fetch — verify against
#     the live backend before relying on auth flows in a store build).
#   - You ran `eas env:list --environment production` and see the variable.
